"[2510.10474v1] When or What? Understanding Consumer Engagement on Digital Platforms (https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.10474v1)\nciteturn2855view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.10474v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 161\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 12 Oct 2025]\nL9: # Title:When or What? Understanding Consumer Engagement on Digital Platforms\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Jingyi Wu , cite6†Junying Liang L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled When or What? Understanding Consumer Engagement on Digital Platforms, by Jingyi Wu and 1 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite7†View PDF cite8†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Understanding what drives popularity is critical in today's digital service economy, where content creators compete for consumer attention. Prior studies have primarily emphasized the role of content features, yet creators often misjudge what audiences actually value. This study applies Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA) modeling to a large corpus of TED Talks, treating the platform as a case of digital service provision in which creators (speakers) and consumers (audiences) interact.\nL17: By comparing the thematic supply of creators with the demand expressed in audience engagement, we identify persistent mismatches between producer offerings and consumer preferences. Our longitudinal analysis further reveals that temporal dynamics exert a stronger influence on consumer engagement than thematic content, suggesting that when content is delivered may matter more than what is delivered.\nL18: These findings challenge the dominant assumption that content features are the primary drivers of popularity and highlight the importance of timing and contextual factors in shaping consumer responses. The results provide new insights into consumer attention dynamics on digital platforms and carry practical implications for marketers, platform managers, and content creators seeking to optimize audience engagement strategies.\nL19: Comments:  | 21 pages, 6 figures, 3 tables\nL20: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Computers and Society (cs.CY)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite9†arXiv:2510.10474 [cs.CL]\nL22:    | (or cite10†arXiv:2510.10474v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL23:    | cite11†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.10474†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Jingyi Wu [cite12†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Sun, 12 Oct 2025 06:53:57 UTC (1,593 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled When or What? Understanding Consumer Engagement on Digital Platforms, by Jingyi Wu and 1 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite7†View PDF L36:   * cite8†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite13†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite14†view license†creativecommons.org L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.CL\nL43: \nL44: cite15†< prev |   cite16†next > L45: \nL46: cite17†new | cite18†recent | cite19†2025-10 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite20†cs L51: cite21†cs.CY L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite22†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite23†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite24†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Button: ×]\nL64: \nL65: loading...\nL66: \nL67: Data provided by:\nL68: \nL69: ### Bookmark\nL70: \nL71: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL72: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL75: \nL